From e07066556d59248a39c298d069e5c8c1bd9e2d19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work_5 <adbtrr_work_5@local>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 11:53:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] apps/examples/testcase: Add semaphore scenario test tier,
 phase 4

Phase 4: families E and F, recovery, plus the read only holder snapshot
command the catalogue specifies for them.

Kernel side, os/:
- os/include/tinyara/os_api_test_drv.h gains TESTIOC_SEM_SNAPSHOT and its
  payload. The holder list, the per holder counts and each holder's base
  priority are kernel structures; under CONFIG_APP_BINARY_SEPARATION a
  user space test cannot reach them, and CONFIG_SEM_PHDEBUG is not
  enabled on the reference platform, so several families had no way to
  see whether a holder record was created, decremented or freed.
- os/drivers/os_api_test/kernel/test_sem.c implements it. Read only,
  allocates nothing, takes a critical section and copies out. The waiter
  count is derived from semcount rather than by walking
  g_waitingforsemaphore: a negative count is by definition the number of
  blocked tasks, so the two agree by construction and the driver does not
  need the scheduler's internal list.

Family E, mutex usage:
- SCN-REC-01 delete a task blocked on a mutex flagged semaphore.
- SCN-REC-02 delete the owner while another task is blocked. The highest
  value oracle in the tier: a recovery that increments the count without
  waking the waiter leaves that task blocked forever behind a semaphore
  whose count now looks available.
- SCN-REC-03 delete a task holding three semaphores, two with waiters.
  Also detects a holder free that fails to unlink from the victim's
  holdsem list, which would spin sem_release_all() forever.
- SCN-REC-05 delete a task holding two counts of one semaphore. A
  measurement scenario: sem_release_all() increments semcount once per
  holder entry, and a holder that took two counts has one entry carrying
  counts == 2. The assertion states the leak free expectation, so a
  failure here is the detection rather than a flaky test, and the drain
  arithmetic is written out in the comment for whoever sees it fail.
- SCN-REC-06 signal and delete racing on one waiter, both orders, 200
  iterations. A count one too high means one path skipped its
  TSTATE_WAIT_SEM guard.

Family F, signalling usage and holder accounting:
- SCN-SIG-01 delete a task blocked on a signalling semaphore, and confirm
  through the snapshot that no holder record ever existed.
- SCN-SIG-02 delete the intended signaller; only the waiter's own timeout
  can recover it, because nothing owns a count to give back.
- SCN-SIG-03 the same with a plain wait. A characterisation scenario: the
  waiter stays blocked, rule G-7 is deliberately waived, and the scenario
  ends by deleting the waiter and checking that is clean. If a future
  change made recovery cover signalling semaphores, this must be updated
  with it rather than silently passing.
- SCN-SIG-04 delete one of two boosted holders. Measured, because
  sem_release_all() does not run the restore pass; what is asserted is
  that the survivor unwinds once it posts.
- SCN-SIG-05 repeated create, acquire and delete cycles, checking through
  the snapshot that the holder record is created and then freed every
  cycle. A container never returned to the free list is invisible until
  the pool is exhausted, at which point inheritance silently stops
  working system wide.

SCN-REC-04, the robust mutex EOWNERDEAD path, is not here: it needs
CONFIG_PTHREAD_MUTEX_ROBUST, which the reference platform does not build.
It ships with phase 5 alongside the dedicated configuration.

Not build verified: no target toolchain in this environment. The
application sources were syntax checked with gcc -Wall -Wextra against
stub headers in three configurations, all clean. The kernel side driver
change is not covered by that check.
